## Secrets

A **Secret** is a tainted string wrapper, a data type of its own. It behaves like
a string in all string functions and the taint propagates through the result, so
every string primitive and every prelude string function carries it along, but it
prints redacted as a hash-and-angle-brackets printout naming only the key. It is
revealed only when serialized into an outgoing MCP call argument or header.

- (secrets) gives an alist of (key . description); values hidden.
- (secret "REPL_TOKEN") gives the value as a tainted string. Only REPL_-prefixed
  keys are visible.
  Example: (load-mcp :name "acme" :url "…" :headers (list (cons "Authorization" (secret "REPL_ACME_TOKEN")))).

### Gotchas

- A secret's printout names its key and not its value, and the reader refuses to
  read one back, so typing one into code is always an error.
- Taint follows the value. Concatenating or splitting a secret gives another
  secret, which is the point: there is no accidental way to launder one into
  plain text.
